  "hints": [
    "Split the paste into small metadata (id, owner, expiry) and a large body. Where does each belong?",
    "Raw bodies are blobs: object storage is cheap and durable. Popular pastes repeat, so cache their metadata.",
    "LB (2) → API (6) → Redis (2) → NoSQL (2) for metadata, and API → S3 for bodies."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 60,
    "costBudget": 1300
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Keep big blobs out of the database: store bodies in object storage and keep only small metadata in a key-value store.",
    "Reads dominate and a few pastes go viral, so a cache in front of the metadata store absorbs most lookups.",
    "Expiry is a TTL on the metadata row plus a lifecycle rule on the bucket; no cron job has to scan everything."
  ]
